EXT. WILLOW GROVE AT THE EDGE OF THE WORLD - TWILIGHT

Weeping willows, silver in the dusk. Fireflies drift between branches like slow sparks. A pond mirrors a sky with two moons. A stone well stands among the roots, its lip furred with moss.

A STRANGER walks into the grove: a woman, ageless, barefoot, wrapped in a patchwork cloak that shifts color as she moves. She carries a lantern with no flame, only a glow like captured dawn. She stops at the well and sets the lantern on its lip.

STRANGER
(to the grove)
I hear you.

The willows rustle. A firefly lands on her sleeve, and she watches it breathe.

STRANGER (CONT'D)
(softly)
I know. I know. It's been a long road.

A voice rises from the well, deep and wet and patient, as though spoken through a thousand years of stone.

VOICE (O.S.)
(from the well)
Who comes to the Mouth of Names?

STRANGER
A traveler.

VOICE (O.S.)
(from the well)
Travelers bear names.

STRANGER
Mine fell off the road a long way back.

She kneels. She dips her hand into the cold dark water. Ripples spread, and the fireflies pause in the air, holding their light.

VOICE (O.S.)
(from the well)
Then I shall give you one.

STRANGER
Give me a good one.

The water brightens, silver then gold. When it clears, the surface shows a face: not hers, but a girl's, laughing.

VOICE (O.S.)
(from the well)
Wren.

The woman closes her eyes. The cloak settles to a single color: the deep green of willow leaves.

WREN
(whispering)
Wren.

She rises, shoulders loose, as though a weight has slid off. She picks up the lantern. Its glow steadies and warms.

WREN (CONT'D)
(to the well)
Thank you.

VOICE (O.S.)
(from the well)
Walk well, Wren.

She leaves the grove. Behind her, the willows close their branches like curtains. The fireflies resume their drift, and the two moons climb.
